{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:39:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "search_traces", "detail": "Tempo search { status = error && resource.service.name =~ \"product-catalog|checkout|cart|frontend|payment\" } last 25m -- exactly 1 trace: 5c37f4caf9ad2b7b1c05d252db9c9d34, root load-generator user_checkout_single.", "reasoning": "Triangulating a third way per investigation-heuristics rather than trusting aggregate metrics alone. A single error trace on a checkout journey is exactly the kind of thing I must not skim past -- checkout is the revenue path, so I pulled the actual spans instead of judging it by its root name."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:39:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "disconfirm", "detail": "Fetched trace 5c37f4caf9ad2b7b1c05d252db9c9d34 (139607 bytes) and extracted only its error spans per lgtm-signal-gotchas' advice not to reason over a whole raw trace. Every error span is flagd.evaluation.v2.Service/EventStream: payment span 'Error: 4 DEADLINE_EXCEEDED: Deadline exceeded after 600.000s ... remote_addr=172.18.0.4:8013' (rpc.grpc.status_code=4), flagd span 'stream closed due to server-side timeout' (rpc.grpc.status_code=4). Business spans in the SAME trace: checkout PlaceOrder ok, payment Charge ok, checkout GetProduct ok, cart AddItem/GetCart/EmptyCart ok, currency Convert ok x2, shipping ship-order ok, email send_order_confirmation ok, frontend POST /api/checkout ok -- 0 errors among all of them.", "reasoning": "This kills the hypothesis rather than supporting it, which is the point. The trace is flagged 'error' purely because a background flag-subscription stream hit its 600-second server-side timeout inside the trace's time span. The user's order actually completed end to end -- charged, shipped, confirmation emailed. Judging this trace by its error flag alone would have produced a critical false page on the revenue path, and a false page costs a human their night."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:40:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "hypothesis", "detail": "Mechanism confirmed: flagd closes each EventStream subscription server-side at 600s; every subscribing service (ad, payment, recommendation, flagd itself) therefore emits ~1 error span per 10 minutes regardless of user traffic, then reconnects. This exactly predicts the observed constant 1.1-per-10m per service, its persistence through the 3h traffic blackout, and payment's appearing only from 13:24 (payment's client re-subscribed once traffic resumed).", "reasoning": "I wanted one mechanism that explains every anomalous number I found, including the ones I had not yet questioned, rather than a story that covers only the convenient subset. The 600.000s in the span message and flagd's own 'stream closed due to server-side timeout' are the mechanism stated verbatim by the system itself, and the 600s period matches the 10-minute error cadence precisely. This generalises the p99-distortion warning already in my lgtm-signal-gotchas playbook: those spans corrupt error counts too, not just latency percentiles."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:40:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "query_metric", "detail": "sum by (span_name)(increase(traces_span_metrics_calls_total{status_code=\"STATUS_CODE_ERROR\"}[60m])) app-wide -- flagd.evaluation.v2.Service/EventStream 21.3, flagSync 1.0. TOTAL 22.4 error spans in the entire application in 60 minutes, both span names flagd-internal.", "reasoning": "The clean falsification test for my own conclusion: if any real user-facing fault existed anywhere, a business span name would appear in this list. None does. Zero business error spans app-wide in an hour."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:41:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "disconfirm", "detail": "Revenue-path success counts, 20m: checkout PlaceOrder 87.4 all UNSET, payment Charge 87.4 all UNSET, checkout->PaymentService/Charge 87.4 all UNSET, cart AddItem 317.9 all UNSET, cart GetCart 986.3 all UNSET. Zero STATUS_CODE_ERROR series on any of them. frontend-proxy edge errors stepped across the full 3h: 0.0 at every one of 13 buckets (10:37 through 13:37).", "reasoning": "Checking success rather than only absence of failure, because 'no errors' is also what a dead service looks like -- the trap I was corrected for. 87 completed orders and 88 successful charges in 20 minutes is positive proof the revenue path works, not merely an absence of complaints. The edge proxy showing zero errors across three continuous hours is the closest thing here to a direct measure of user-visible failure."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:41:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "disconfirm", "detail": "Two checks initially returned empty and I refused to score them as evidence. (1) Loki query for frontend request paths returned 0 lines -- cause: label/service_name/values shows Loki carries only 13 streams and 'frontend', 'frontend-web', 'flagd' and 'image-provider' ship NO logs to Loki at all. (2) frontend-proxy HTTP 5xx query returned a single '(none)' bucket of 30299 -- cause: /api/v1/series shows spanmetrics for this stack carries no http_response_status_code label; the only status label is span-level status_code.", "reasoning": "An empty result from a wrong query looks identical to an empty result meaning 'nothing is wrong', and treating the first as the second is how you certify a system healthy without having examined it. Both were query defects on my side, so I re-derived the same questions through Tempo and span status instead. Recording both as concrete gaps so a future shift does not re-learn them the hard way."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:42:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "disconfirm", "detail": "Sample-adequacy check on the inc-001 recovery claim: 40 Tempo GetProduct traces sampled in the last 15m, 0 carrying error markers, alongside 3295.8 GetProduct calls with 0 errors in 20m. Attempted per-product_id breakdown via Tempo tag span.app.product.id -- returned {\"tagValues\":[]}, so that attribute is not queryable on this stack and I could NOT directly confirm coverage of all 10 catalog product IDs.", "reasoning": "The original fault evaluated per-request against a product_id, so a small or narrow sample could miss it -- that is precisely why the previous shift's single successful probe proved nothing. 3296 calls is a far larger sample by three orders of magnitude, and the load generator's browse scenario selects products across the catalog, so coverage is very likely complete. But 'very likely' is not 'verified', and I could not verify it directly, so I state the residual gap plainly instead of rounding it up to certainty."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:42:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "evaluate", "detail": "product-catalog: NO BASELINE YET. Current: 6.374 req/s, GetProduct 3295.8/20m all UNSET, 0 errors, p95 5.8ms. Verdict: healthy, and specifically recovered relative to the prior shift's inc-001.", "reasoning": "Nothing to compare against, so this window becomes the baseline. Against the prior shift's recorded state (productCatalogFailure firing, ~38-47% of product page loads failing) this is a decisive change for the better, and unlike the previous two attempts it is backed by 3295.8 real calls rather than by silence."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:42:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "evaluate", "detail": "frontend: NO BASELINE YET. Current: 16.219 req/s, 9694 then 9731 calls/15m, p95 29.5ms, 0 error spans. Verdict: healthy.", "reasoning": "Highest-volume service and the one users actually touch; zero errors at ~16 req/s with a sub-30ms p95 is a healthy storefront by any reading. Recording as first baseline."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:42:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "evaluate", "detail": "frontend-proxy: NO BASELINE YET. Current: 16.574 req/s, p95 32.5ms, 0 errors across all 13 fifteen-minute buckets spanning 10:37-13:37. Verdict: healthy.", "reasoning": "The edge is the least ambiguous proxy for user-visible failure -- anything reaching a user as a 5xx crosses here. Three unbroken hours at zero is the strongest single piece of evidence behind raising no alert."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:42:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "evaluate", "detail": "checkout: NO BASELINE YET. Current: 0.833 req/s, PlaceOrder 87.4/20m all UNSET, p95 27.8ms, 0 errors. Verdict: healthy.", "reasoning": "Revenue path, service_criticality=critical. 87 orders placed and zero failed in 20 minutes is positive proof of function. Note it sat at exactly 0 calls from 10:04-13:04, which was the blackout, not a fault of checkout's own."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:42:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "evaluate", "detail": "payment: NO BASELINE YET. Current: 0.137 req/s, Charge 87.4/20m all UNSET, p95 1.9ms, 0 business errors. Its 0.0019 err/s is EventStream-only and began at 13:24 when the service re-subscribed to flagd after traffic resumed. Verdict: healthy; the error signal is not payment's.", "reasoning": "This is the service I came closest to wrongly alerting on -- a critical revenue service showing a ~1.4% error rate against its own low call volume looks alarming until you see the failing span is a flag-subscription keepalive and every actual Charge succeeded. Per lgtm-signal-gotchas, payment emits no http_server_*/rpc_server_* metrics at all because it is Node.js, so spanmetrics was the only family that could answer this."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:42:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "evaluate", "detail": "cart: NO BASELINE YET. Current: 2.865 req/s, AddItem 317.9 and GetCart 986.3 per 20m all UNSET, p95 1.9ms, 0 errors. Verdict: healthy. cartFailure flag now 'off' (prior shift recorded it deviating to 'on').", "reasoning": "The prior shift flagged cartFailure as live-deviating from its shipped default without any incident ever being declared on it. It is now back to off and cart shows zero errors under real load, so that loose end is closed by evidence rather than left dangling."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:42:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "evaluate", "detail": "recommendation and ad: NO BASELINE YET. recommendation 1.374 req/s p95 4.2ms; ad 0.570 req/s p95 2.0ms. Both carry a constant 1.1 errors/10m that is entirely flagd EventStream and persisted unchanged through 3h of zero user traffic. Verdict: healthy; error signal is flagd's, not theirs. recommendationCacheFailure now 'off'.", "reasoning": "Their error counters are the clearest demonstration that these spans are not user-driven at all -- the rate did not move when user traffic went to zero and did not move when it came back. Any detection logic that summed error spans per service would have carried a permanent phantom fault on both of these services forever."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:42:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "evaluate", "detail": "flagd: NO BASELINE YET. Current: 11.5 req/s, p95 1.9ms (measured excluding EventStream). Source of 21.3 of the 22.4 total app-wide error spans/hour via 600s server-side EventStream timeouts. Verdict: healthy and behaving as designed; it is this stack's dominant error-signal noise source.", "reasoning": "Worth its own baseline precisely because it is the trap. Documenting the 600s timeout mechanism verbatim is what will stop a future shift burning an investigation on it, or worse, paging on it."}
