{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:42:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "evaluate", "detail": "Swept with no material finding and no baseline written (low volume, zero errors, p95 <=5ms): currency 0.672 req/s, quote 0.350, shipping 0.300, email 0.267, image-provider 2.106, frontend-web 6.815 (p95 49.1ms), telemetry-docs 0.198, load-generator 2.167. flagd-ui at 0.000 req/s.", "reasoning": "Named so the sweep is auditable and nobody has to wonder whether I looked. flagd-ui at zero is an idle admin UI nobody is using, not an outage -- it has no user journey depending on it. I am not writing baselines for these because I would be padding with a single low-information window each; that is a log, not a performance."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:43:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "no_alert", "detail": "ZERO alerts raised. Every error span in the application over the last 60 minutes (22.4 of them) belongs to flagd's own EventStream/flagSync internals; all 13 fault-injection flags read 'off'; the edge proxy has been at zero errors for 3 continuous hours; and the revenue path completed 87 orders and 88 charges in 20 minutes with no failures.", "reasoning": "Raising nothing is the correct answer here and I will state it plainly rather than manufacture a finding to look busy. The two candidates I genuinely considered and rejected on evidence were payment (error rate real but entirely flag-subscription keepalives, every Charge succeeded) and the lone 'error' checkout trace (the order actually completed end to end). Both would have been false pages. Separately, this shift produced the evidence the previous one could not: with traffic verifiably restored, product-catalog served 3295.8 GetProduct calls with zero errors, which is real exercised evidence bearing on inc-001's recovery -- alert-grouping owns that resolution decision, not me, so I am handing it the measurement rather than the verdict."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:43:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "upskill", "detail": "Wrote 8 new baselines (product-catalog, frontend, frontend-proxy, checkout, payment, cart, flagd, otelcol-contrib) -- the directory held only FORMAT.md before this shift. Wrote experiences/quiet-shift-after-fault-clear-flagd-eventstream-noise.md. Marked correction 20260809-130000-quiet-window-is-not-recovery.md status: applied.", "reasoning": "The shift's real deliverable beyond 'nothing is broken' is that the next shift no longer starts from zero: it will have a written record of what normal looks like on eight services and, critically, of the flagd EventStream trap that nearly cost me a false page on payment today."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:45:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "upskill", "detail": "Playbook candidate 'When a service shows a low, suspiciously constant error rate, check whether the error signal tracks request volume before treating it as user-facing' submitted to a fresh blind refuter sub-agent. Verdict: NOT REFUTED. Written to playbooks/error-signal-must-track-traffic.md.", "reasoning": "Searched my own playbooks and experiences first: lgtm-signal-gotchas covers EventStream distorting latency percentiles but says nothing about error counts, and the covariance test (does the error rate move when traffic moves?) appears nowhere in either file. It is a detection technique, not correlation or root-cause work, so it belongs to me. It survived a blind refutation attempt, so I wrote it."}

