{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:36:30Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "query_metric", "detail": "increase(traces_span_metrics_calls_total{service_name=~\"frontend|product-catalog|cart|checkout\"}[10m]) stepped 15m over 4h. product-catalog: 0 calls at every step 10:04->13:04, then 4028 (13:19) and 3824 (13:34). checkout: 0 from 10:04->13:04, then 638 and 500. frontend: ~40 during the same dead period, then 9694 and 9731. cart: ~40, then 1909 and 1718.", "reasoning": "This is the correction applied literally. The storefront was genuinely dark from ~10:04 to ~13:04 UTC -- product-catalog and checkout served ZERO requests, and frontend's residual ~40/15min was not user traffic. Traffic resumed between 13:04 and 13:19 UTC, consistent with loadGeneratorTraffic being back 'on'. This matters enormously for what I am allowed to conclude: any statement about inc-001 having recovered is only meaningful using data from 13:19 onward, because before that nothing was exercising the code path. I am deliberately basing every recovery claim below on the post-13:19 window only."}
{"ts": "2026-08-09T13:37:00Z", "agent": "log-triage", "action": "query_metric", "detail": "histogram_quantile(0.95, ...duration_milliseconds_bucket{span_name!=\"flagd.evaluation.v2.Service/EventStream\"}) -- frontend-web 49.1ms, frontend-proxy 32.5ms, frontend 29.5ms, checkout 27.8ms, product-catalog 5.8ms, recommendation 4.2ms, cart 1.9ms, payment 1.9ms. load-generator 2344.1ms.", "reasoning": "Errors are not the only failure mode -- a system can be fully 'up' and unusable. I excluded flagd's EventStream span explicitly because lgtm-signal-gotchas warns those are long-lived streaming spans that dominate a percentile and would have manufactured a fake latency incident. load-generator's 2344ms is its own scenario wall-time (a simulated user journey including think-time), not a service responding slowly, so I do not read it as latency."}
